-### Map edges
-
-Something has to stop you riding off the side of the line, with no invisible walls and no warning popups. The pick is that each region chooses its own edge, and the two sides of the line can be different: a canyon region could have a mesa wall on one side and a great river on the other. You should be able to see an edge coming before you reach it: heat shimmer over the desert, a line of mesas on the horizon, the sound of the river. Camps sit inside the edges.
-
-The first edges come in G10, because the map already has everything they need (deep water, cliffs, blocked, slow and mud ground):
-
-| Edge | How it stops you | Kind |
-|---|---|---|
-| Great river | Wide deep water, with fords only near the line | Hard wall |
-| Mesa wall | A long cliff or row of mesas blocks the whole side | Hard wall |
-| Mountain range | Snowy peaks rise to impassable rock | Hard wall |
-| Deep canyon | A gorge runs beside the line; the far rim can't be reached | Hard wall |
-| Badlands | Broken ground, mud and rock: very slow, and nothing out there | Soft |
-| Marsh or bog | Mud everywhere slows you to a crawl | Soft |
-
-The others need systems that come later, so they arrive with those:
-
-| Edge | How it stops you | Arrives with |
-|---|---|---|
-| Endless desert | No water; your horse tires and slows until it won't go on | A horse stamina step after G10 |
-| Dense forest | Slow riding, and the trees hide train smoke on the horizon | G9 (smoke) |
-| Salt flats | Fast and open, but with no cover chasers spot you from far off | Pinkertons, after Phase 3 |
-| The law's reach | The further from the line, the more posses ride | G11-G12 (bounty hunters, wanted level per region) |
-| Ranch land, army territory | Fences and ranchers who shoot; patrols that raise your wanted level | After Phase 3 combat |
-| Wild animals | Wolves or rattlesnakes spook the horse and can throw you | After G10 (a horse spook and throw) |
-| Dust storm wall | A storm front you can't see or steer in turns you back | Weather or night, after G8 |
-
-**Pinkertons** aren't scheduled yet. They need guards who ride and fight, so they come with or after Phase 3 combat, and they get better with G10's terrain.
-
-## Multiplayer
-
-Riding as a gang with friends is part of the plan from the start ("multiplayer-ready by design" in the requirements). It comes in two steps (decided 2026-10-27):
-- **A network test right after Phase 3.** Two players on one train through a simple server, with no lobby. Combat is the hardest part to get right over a network (shots, melee, guards reacting to several players), so it gets tested as soon as it exists. The test keeps running as Phases 4 and 5 add the passenger and bank trains, so each new train is checked over the network as it's built.
-- **Full multiplayer at the end, after the prototype.** Lobbies, accounts, lag handling and the rest come then.
-
-**Already in place** (since Phase 0, so adding it later isn't a rewrite):
-- Every command and everything a player owns carries a player id. The run state is split into shared parts (countdown, heat, the train) and per-player parts (health, carried loot).
-- The game logic has no global state, so one server can host several games.
-- Commands are stamped with the tick they happen on, and the host owns the clock.
-- The state is plain data, so snapshots are cheap to send.
-- The model is a server in charge with prediction on each player's machine (not lockstep), so small differences between browsers only cause small corrections.
-
-**Still to build:** sending the game over the network, lobbies, handling lag, cheat protection, accounts and per-player saves.
-
-**Design questions still open:** how loot is split, whose heat counts, and where saves live once there are accounts.
-
-**Where it shows up:**
-- **After Phase 3:** the network test, kept working through Phases 4 and 5.
-- **After the prototype:** full online multiplayer.
-- **G15, the daily day:** owners can ride the daily as a gang with friends who don't own the game, and an occasional free gang weekend lets free players ride together.
-- **G16, shared world online:** your gang shares one day and camp, a server checks the chunks near each player, and Pinkertons split up to chase each player.
-
-## How the tracks fit together
-
-The G series and the phases mostly don't touch, so they can be worked on side by side. The network test waits on Phase 3, and full multiplayer on the prototype. G4 to G10 can go ahead at any time. These steps wait on phase work:
-- G11 (careers) needs Phase 2 M5 saving.
-- G13 (regions) needs more train types from Phases 3 to 5.
-- G15's gang daily and G16 (shared world) need online multiplayer.
-
-## After Phase 3
-
-- **The network test.** Two players on one train over a simple server (see Multiplayer above). It comes before Phase 4, and each later phase checks its train with it.
 - **Phase 4: the passenger hold-up.** Reading passengers by sight, an inspect action, and armed passengers. Gate: judging passengers is a decision, not a chore.
 - **Phase 5: the bank train.** Cracking the vault while guards interrupt. Gate: the minigame holds up under time pressure, and each train clearly favours a different playstyle.
-- **After the prototype.** Cargo and tycoon trains, the later trains, progression, 3D graphics and full online multiplayer.
 
